      <div>
        <h2>What is included in the price?</h2>
        <p class="brx-answer">Everything needed for a safe, finished install. Our fitted price includes:</p>
        <ul class="bi-checks">
          {included.map(([h, t]) => <li><strong>{h}.</strong> {t}</li>)}
        </ul>
        <p class="brx-small">After the install, Gas Safe Register sends you a Building Regulations Compliance Certificate by post. Keep it with the Benchmark checklist. You will need both if you sell the house.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="br-photo">
        <picture>
          <source type="image/webp" srcset="/images/jobs/boiler-smart-thermostat-fitted-480.webp 480w, /images/jobs/boiler-smart-thermostat-fitted-960.webp 960w" sizes="(max-width: 800px) 100vw, 300px" />
          <img src="/images/jobs/boiler-smart-thermostat-fitted-960.jpg" alt="Boiler and smart thermostat receiver fitted by our team in a Liverpool home" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="960" height="1280" />
        </picture>
        <figcaption>A recent job: boiler controls fitted by our team.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- REPLACE OR REPAIR -->
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="replace">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do you need a new boiler, or just a repair?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Not every broken boiler needs replacing. These are the signs that a new one is the better buy:</p>
      <div class="brx-parts">
        {signs.map(([h, t]) => <div class="brx-part"><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <div class="brx-note">
        <p><strong>Not sure?</strong> Most boilers last 10 to 15 years. The age is on the data label, often inside the front cover, or on the Benchmark checklist. A common rule of thumb: if one repair would cost more than half the price of a new boiler, replace it. If the boiler is under 10 years old and one part has failed, a repair is usually better value. Book a <a href="/boiler-repair-liverpool">boiler repair</a> visit (from £120 for the first hour, including diagnosis) and we will tell you honestly which makes sense.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- TYPES -->
  <section class="brx-sec" id="type">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which boiler do you need: combi, system or regular?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">It depends on how many bathrooms you have, your water pressure and the system you already have.</p>
      <div class="brx-types">
        <div><h3>Combi</h3><p>Heats water straight from the mains when you turn a tap on. No cylinder or loft tanks. Best for most homes with one bathroom. <strong>From £2,195.</strong></p></div>
        <div><h3>System</h3><p>Stores hot water in a cylinder, with no tank in the loft. Good for homes with two or more bathrooms in use at once. <strong>Priced at the free survey.</strong></p></div>
        <div><h3>Regular</h3><p>Also called conventional or heat-only. Uses a cylinder and a cold water tank in the loft. Often the simplest swap in older homes. <strong>Priced at the free survey.</strong></p></div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">A combi needs good mains water pressure and flow. We measure both at the survey before recommending one.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SIZE -->
  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="size">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What size boiler do you need?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">Boiler size is its output in kilowatts (kW). For a combi, size depends mostly on how much hot water you need at once, not the number of radiators.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table bi-size">
          <thead><tr><th>Your home</th><th>Usual size</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{sizes.map(([h, s]) => <tr><td>{h}</td><td><strong>{s}</strong></td></tr>)}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>These are a guide. Too small and the shower runs lukewarm. Too big and the boiler wastes gas switching on and off. We work out the right size at the survey.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- BRANDS -->
  <section class="brx-sec" id="brands">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Which boiler brands do you fit?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We fit four of the most popular UK brands. Every boiler we fit is A-rated, with 7 to 12 years of manufacturer cover depending on the model.</p>
      <div class="brx-parts">
        {brands.map(([h, t]) => <div class="brx-part"><h3>{h}</h3><p>{t}</p></div>)}
      </div>
      <h3 style="margin-top:1.5rem;">What keeps the warranty valid?</h3>
      <p>Two things. The boiler must be registered with the manufacturer, which we do for you on install day. And it must be serviced every year by a Gas Safe registered engineer. Miss a service and the maker can refuse a claim. Our <a href="/boiler-service-liverpool">annual boiler service</a> is £120 and updates your Benchmark record.</p>
      <h3>Is a big-name brand worth paying more for?</h3>
      <p>For most homeowners planning to stay 10 years or more, yes. You get a longer warranty, and parts stay easy to get as the boiler ages, so repairs are quicker. All four brands we fit are well supported in Liverpool. Lesser-known brands can be cheaper to buy, but parts can be harder to find later. We will talk you through the options at the survey.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

